00:00:01.829 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
this morning they were still fighting  the blaze at the zaparilla nuclear plant  the fire missed the reactors themselves  but this latest video shows just how  close the shelling came  rounds punching holes through the  walkway running through the nuclear  complex just meters from the reactors  themselves  a potentially grave nuclear incident was  avoided but it's still a tragedy of a  different kind officials had warned of  the nuclear risks since this invasion  began  warnings that have been ignored we of  course are fortunate that there was no  release of radiation but  it is obvious that when we all agree on  these principles  words must mean something  and we have to act in consequence there  are six reactors at the zaparisia  complex each containing 60 tons of  uranium fuel fortunately the part of the  complex that caught fire was this  training facility a few hundred meters  from the reactors themselves at the time  of the incident only this reactor number  four was operating the other units had  already been shut down as precaution  making them much safer were they to be  damaged  in addition to zaparisia in the east  there are three other nuclear complexes  in ukraine rivner  helmanitzki and south ukraine nuclear  power plant there's concern about this  facility too given the expected russian  advance on odessa to the south  what's the risk the concern of course is  a chernobyl style nuclear accident the  worst in the industry's history  but all ukraine's current reactors are  of the same vver model it's a design  that's inherently safer than chernobyl  they have a thick steel pressure vessel  around the reactor and a concrete  containment building protecting the  entire structure but they're not  designed to withstand warfare modern  reactors are actually very strong and  robust and before that time they do have  containment and they do have some  structural integrity but  i mean whether they've been designed  with projectiles and missiles in mind i  think that's probably not the case  experts believe the worst case scenario  for a reactor failure in ukraine would  be something similar to the fukushima  disaster in japan  prompt evacuation meant no known  fatalities linked to radiation there  but a nuclear emergency during a  conflict already as brutal as this  carries untold risk  the fact that ukraine's nuclear sites  are even in the firing line says much  about the invading army's attitude  towards the harm they might cause  tom clark sky news
 

